TI  - Malnutrition and Gastrointestinal and Respiratory Infections in Children: A Public Health Problem
AU  - Leonor Rodrı́guez
AU  - Elsa Cervantes
AU  - Rocı́o Ortiz
JO  - International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health
PY  - 2011
VL  - 8
IS  - 4
SP  - 1174
EP  - 1205
DO  - 10.3390/ijerph8041174
AN  - 21695035
AB  - Infectious disease is the major cause of morbidity and mortality in developing countries, particularly in children. Increasing evidence suggests that protein-calorie malnutrition is the underlying reason for the increased susceptibility to infections observed in these areas. Moreover, certain infectious diseases also cause malnutrition, which can result in a vicious cycle. Malnutrition and bacterial gastrointestinal and respiratory infections represent a serious public health problem. The increased incidence and severity of infections in malnourished children is largely due to the deterioration of immune function; limited production and/or diminished functional capacity of all cellular components of the immune system have been reported in malnutrition. In this review, we analyze the cyclical relationship between malnutrition, immune response dysfunction, increased susceptibility to infectious disease, and metabolic responses that further alter nutritional status. The consequences of malnutrition are diverse and included: increased susceptibility to infection, impaired child development, increased mortality rate and individuals who come to function in suboptimal ways.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Vitamin A Deficiency: Health, Survival, and Vision
AU  - Alfred Sommer
AU  - Keith P. West
JO  - Archives of Ophthalmology
PY  - 1998
VL  - 116
IS  - 3
SP  - 404
EP  - 404
DO  - 10.1001/archopht.116.3.404
AB  - Vitamin A deficiency is one of the most serious and widespread nutritional disorders of children in developing countries. Knowledge about the important role of vitamin A in human nutrition has progressed remarkably in the past decade, and studies have convincingly demonstrated that vitamin A deficiency, even before it is clinically evident, contributes to high fatality rates among young children. Specialized agencies of the United Nations have raised their level of commitment, and several international forums have called for the virtual elimination of vitamin A deficiency as a public health problem by the end of this century. In this clearly presented volume, Sommer and West provide an overview of the main aspects of the vitamin A deficiency problem with particular attention to epidemiology and public health management of deficient populations. Historical development, relation of vitamin A to child survival, infectious morbidity, anemia and growth, biochemistry, immunology, and the pathophysiology of xerophthalmia are also thoroughly treated. The first chapter retraces the vitamin A deficiency problem from an historical point of view and further raises questions about the late interest of researchers in the significance of "mild deficiency" as an important determinant of child survival and for the systemic consequences of vitamin A deficiency. Chapters 2-6 summarize very clearly and completely our present knowledge of the consequences of vitamin A deficiency in child survival, infectious morbidity (especially diarrhea, measles, and respiratory disease), xerophthalmia, anemia, and growth. A large number of published as well as unpublished studies are discussed. Attention is drawn to the still unclear action mechanisms of vitamin A and to important areas of research for the future. Chapters 7-9 deal with more physiologic aspects. Chapter 7 reviews the many factors that contribute to the appearance of vitamin A deficiency, the most important of which are the systemic infections, diarrhea, respiratory disease, and measles, and proteinenergy malnutrition. However, the authors insist on our still limited understanding of molecular mechanisms by which protein status affects vitamin Adependent functions. Chapter 8 describes the physiologic and biochemical processes that underly vitamin A activity and show the recent progress and growing interest in this matter since the identification in 1987 of nuclear receptors for retinoic acid that induce gene expression. Chapter 9 focuses on the relation between immunocompetence and vitamin A status, an area of knowledge that is rapidly expanding. Although the exact role of vitamin A in maintaining immunocompetence has yet to be made clear, there is strong evidence that vitamin A plays an important part in maintaining the lymphocyte pool, in T-cell-mediated responses and in stimulating nonspecific immunity. Chapter 10, which deals with treatment, reviews the different patterns for the management of severely as well as mildly vitamin A deficient children. Effectiveness of vitamin A supplementation, dosage, and frequencies are discussed. Efficacy of high-dose supplementation in children with measles is highlighted and response in children with various degrees of malnutrition is debated. Chapter 11 summarizes the different techniques for assessing vitamin A status. Although considerable progress has been made in the past 10 years to develop field-applicable assessment methodologies, none of the available indices are free of limitations that restrict their interpretation in some settings. The review of the epidemiology of vitamin A deficiency reminds us of the magnitude of this public health problem, particularly in the southern regions of the world. Worldwide, 5 to 10 million children develop xerophthalmia each year, and half of them go blind, whereas 190 to 240 million children have been estimated to be at risk of subclinical vitamin A deficiency. Xerophthalmia particularly affects the most vulnerable groups in the lower socioeconomic strata, and it tends to cluster within provinces and even households. This underlines the need to target communities of greatest need in prevention programs. Peak age often occurs from 2-4 years of age and the protective effect of breastfeeding against clinical xerophthalmia has been well demonstrated. Supplement-
ER  - 

